1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:03,920
Can you spot the two characters we can drop?

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,560
The backticks on either side of in

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:07,280
just disappear.

4
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:11,950
The idea first came up in November 2015.

5
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:17,680
It was implemented that December and shipped with Swift 2.2 the following March.

6
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,460
Suppose we want to find a value in a list.

7
00:00:21,910 --> 00:00:23,670
Let's read the function name aloud.

8
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:26,680
Index of a value in a collection.

9
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:30,490
The name tells us what to find and where to look.

10
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:35,460
Since in was a reserved word, it needed backticks here.

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,560
Look at the second argument on the left.

12
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:42,090
The code on the right passes the same list.

13
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:46,020
Only the two backticks around in have changed.

14
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:48,790
The old call still works.

15
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:52,250
Notice this phrase in the proposal title.

16
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,140
Most keywords.

17
00:00:54,590 --> 00:00:56,590
That means most of the language's keywords.

18
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,440
Which ones were left out?

19
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:02,330
inout, var, let.

20
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:06,940
At the time, these also modified parameters in declarations.

21
00:01:07,390 --> 00:01:11,630
So as labels there, they still needed backticks.

22
00:01:12,930 --> 00:01:15,730
The proposal also mentioned another name.

23
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:16,980
within.

24
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:19,750
It was a way to avoid the backticks.

25
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,440
With Swift 2.2, the shorter in became an option.

26
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:29,740
In this function, in tells us where to look for the value.

27
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:34,910
Next time, we'll see why Swift removed its curried declaration syntax.
